alarm bells here in germany as book  talks at two university campuses were  canceled this week allegedly due to  pressure from china the online events  were to mark the publication of a new  biography xi jinping the most powerful  man in the world co-authored by two  german journalists but the publisher  told local media that the talks were  abruptly called off after chinese  interference including from the  consulate in dusseldorf  the book talks in hanover and duesberg  were initially sponsored by chinese  centers known as confucius institutes  which are affiliated with the german  campuses both university administrations  say they were not involved in the  planning of the events but are concerned  about the cancellations in a statement  the university of duisburg essen said  quote the administration is particularly  disturbed that official pressure was  apparently used to stop this event we  cannot understand this decision and it  cannot be allowed to happen again  we're joined now by one of the authors  of the book adrian gigas who joins us  from hamburg welcome to the program now  you were meant to be at both of these  book events but they were cancelled can  you tell us what happened  so a few days before the event  we got contacted by the confucius  institutes and they said oh we are very  sorry we would like to do it and we  announced it of course  but  we got an order from beijing  not to do it  and i asked why i mean this  is a book about china which tells it  from all sides it's not a china  bashing book  and they said yeah the problem is  according to their chinese counterparts  it is a book about xi jinping and they  don't want this person to be touched and  discussed and that's why they hadn't  been allowed to do it  but originally they were on board to do  the event and then they said they got a  call or some kind of contact from  beijing that said please cancel this  event  exactly so  they had been very sorry and they said  we got this pressure that even that  they got the pressure not directly from  their counterparts at the chinese  universities you know it's always a  corporation  german university and chinese university  in fact their counterparts in the  chinese universities had agreed as well  to these events but they got pressure  from the top  not to do it and that their counterpart  they are even fried frightened and even  after they explained now this is  very harmful for the confucius institute  in germany that said  we don't care this is too dangerous  right and as you said you you mentioned  that your book is looking at the  president from all sides it's not a book  that only praises him you're a little  bit critical as well do you feel that's  the reason why this event was cancelled  yeah i mean  what i'm feeling is and what they even  told us it's because you know there's  such a  cult of personality around xi jinping  now in china  and even to portray him as a human being  is too much i mean even to write  simple facts  he was divorced or something like this  we don't care about it we only mentioned  it in a sentence in the book  even this is too much because they will  uh show him in china as something like  like a superstar or even like like a god  and so to show him as a normal person  and to show the personal history of his  life is already too much for them  now in response to these cancellations  the universities themselves have  scheduled their own events about your  book without the confucius institutes  one of the events was last night why do  you think it's important that these  events take place  oh very important because i mean what it  was it was an attempt  of censorship so to censor not only in  china  itself as we know it but  to  censor also in germany and in other  countries and luckily there had been a  lot of protests in the last days a lot  of media attention  media a lot of media reported about it  and this is the reason why  because it was embarrassing for the  universities as well why the university  universities decided to hold these  events  adrian gigas the co-author of xi jinping  the most powerful man in the world thank  you very much for your time  we reached out to both confucius  institutes for comment here's how the  hanover center explained its  cancellation there were differences of  opinion with our chinese partners this  meant it was no longer possible to  retain the format and for the confucius  institute to be involved  joining me now for more is thorsten  benner he's the co-founder and director  of the global public policy institute  here in berlin thurston thanks for  taking the time let's talk a bit about  the confucius institutes here in germany  how do they operate and what kind of  links do they have to the universities  in this country  thank you for having me abby confucius  institutes are an interesting animal  because unlike for example the german  goethe institutes  they're not operating just independently  in a in the third country but we have  more than a dozen confucius institutes  that are on the one hand co-funded and  co-run by  chinese party state institutions but  they're part and affiliated and co-run  by german universities that are  committed to academic freedom and that's  just not a match  academic freedom you say academic  independence should german universities  then be linked with these institutes  no i don't think they should the  solution is actually pretty simple just  cut them loose from german german  universities and have them just operate  independently and the german government  should  invest massively in independent chinese  culture and language competence and  build that up at german universities  they're simply no match it's not for no  reason that in the academic freedom  index china ranks to the very bottom we  cannot accept  that  to have chinese funding and  institutions that are co-run  by beijing affiliated with german  universities that's simply no match it's  a massive reputational risk as we've  seen also in the recent case of these  cancer lectures where we had direct  interference from beijing on on this  and  but as i said the solution is pretty  simple and  confucius institutes are one example of  the problematic tie-ups  with the the chinese  academic system in in germany but  they're by far not the biggest challenge  i found now beijing is not timid about  calling out instances it feels is  interference by other states has  chancellor merkel and her governments or  successive governments done enough to  push back on beijing  no i think we've seen a turning of the  tide there's  there's quite a quite a few politicians  who are calling out the problems uh  involved with if you accept chinese  funding if you have confucius institutes  the risks of self-censorship of economic  espionage in academic cooperation with  beijing but the chancellor herself she  hasn't done much to the contrary earlier  this year we had a case where beijing  slapped sanctions on german independent  researchers and research institutes such  as the marcato institute for china  studies the biggest research center on  china in in europe and all we got  from the chancery was silence and that's  not acceptable  there's not i do want to ask you then  we're going to see a new coalition  government here in germany what could or  should they do differently when it comes  to beijing  i think they they should match the risks  much better and  cut any  any dependence on chinese funding in the  german academic sector german professors  shouldn't have any business taking  consulting fees and from from from china  and they should speak out for academic  freedom and  be uncompromising  uncompromising on that they should  encourage collaboration but manage the  risk much better and i do think we have  a  firm basis for that the greens and the  free democrats have spoken up very  clearly about  the risks of academic freedom not being  kind of held in high regard by beijing  and the risk of the cooperation and  there are quite a few in the in the  social democrats uh who are kind of  pushing  for this also in in merkel's party many  don't buy her a pro i i do think we're  in  we have an opportunity for change on on  on this front and restructuring the  relationship so potentially  a turning of the tide with with the new  government there thorsten banner  director of the global public policy  institute thank you for your insights  thurston
 

